From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 10:27:48 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-471] ETHICS: "Sleeping" for success & virtue

At 10:56 AM 12/9/94, Max More wrote:
>I see sex and relationships as
>quite difficult to separate. You said you agreed with the undesirability of
>faking relationships. It could be hard to separate the two.

Robert Anton Wilson calls this the "argument from insufficient imagination"
-- "I can't imagine something, therefore it can't be."  Clearly, a great
many other folks can and do separate sex and relationships all the time.
Is there any reason why they are philosophically incorrect in doing so?

>To me, it isn't
>simply a matter of sex being a different status from other things you can
>exchange for desired ends (or exchanging your desired end for things you
>want!). I think offering something else for your goal could be just as wrong.
>For instance, if I offer to have your rival killed or their character
>publicly smeared in return for your promoting me. I'm concerned primarily
>with someone's character, the kind of person they choose to be.

Egad!  Are you lumping fucking in with force and fraud?  How unlibertarian!
To equate a friendly roll in the hay with murder and slander is deeply
repellant to me -- are you sure you don't want to rethink this position?

>In Madonna's case that seems to be true. In the case of most people their
>reputation would be more likely to suffer more.

Do you really think so?  Victorian attitudes aside, how many occupations
nowadays really depend on a reputation for sexual chastity?  Looking
backward, none of the various positions (no pun intended) I have held have
ever depended in any way on my sexual behavior, pro or con.  Would you
consider Ayn Rand a less qualified philosopher because she had a liaison
with a married man?  Would you discount Carl Sagan's scientific
achievements if he had shacked up with Ann Druyan before divorcing Lynn
Margulis?  (Did he?  I dunno; I don't care.)  How can the details of my sex
life possibly diminish my effectiveness as a technical editor or author?

>In any case, this talk of
>reputation capital seems rather beside the point in my view, as does your
>saying the rival who gives up the trophy for sex must want the sex more
>than the title. In my view it can make good sense to say that someone's
>wants are unhealthy and bad for them.

Certainly.  But as a libertarian, it's none of my business to decide this
for them.  They must decide for themselves.

>I take it that you do not want to say
>this, and would be willing to grant anyone's instrumentally rational actions
>as good for them.

No; of course I recognize that people sometimes choose, on the basis of a
sound (though perhaps short-sighted) rationalization, actions that are bad
for them in the long term.  However, not being in their shoes, I am in no
position to judge the consequences of their actions for them.  So long as
it appears to both of us that the current course of action suits both of
our interests -- if it appears to grant both of us both our short-term and
long-term wants -- then where's the problem?  Are you placing a burden of
omniscience on sexual persons, that they must refrain from fucking unless
they possess absolute certainty that no harm will come to their partner as
a result of it?

>> To my mind, selling sex is no different than selling any other commodity
>> you may have.
>
>Would you also sell your principles, and your friends and family? These,
>too, are commodities. If the answer is no, then you _do_ consider some

I don't consider any of these commodities.  For one thing, I don't own my
friends and family.  I do own my principles, but, unlike sex, once I sell
them, I don't have them any more.  Sex, on the other hand, is, like
information, arbitrarily replicable -- ya got it; ya sell it; ya still got
it.

>aspects of your self and your relationships not as commodities. The
>difference between our attitudes would then be then I hold more things
>as non-commodities.

Now we get to the heart of the issue:  what are your grounds for
considering sex a non-commodity?

>> From: plaz@netcom.com (Plaz)
>
>> What does bother me is the absolutist moral sense in which his argument was
>> phrased. Referrences to cheating in sports and false relationships, etc are
>> all taking the extremes of the issue and dealing with them as if it were
>> the entire issue. [...]
>
>It seems to me that the same principles are involved in these cases. You
>haven't shown otherwise, so I think it reasonable to bring these cases into
>account.

Perhaps the distinction wasn't made clear:  those other cases involve
fraud.  So long as a freely-given sexual liaison doesn't have fraudulent
intent, what's wrong with it?

>I espouse an ethics of virtue (as
>distinguished from a consequentialist or deontological morality). On an
>ethics of virtue, character is primary rather than actions, outcomes, or
>abstract rules. The virtuous person is one who lives in accordance with their
>nature. Living virtuously means _flourishing_. The basic moral facts are
>those about what is constitutively and instrumentally needed for life as the
>kind of being we are, that is, for a flourishing life.

What is your evidence for considering trade in sexual favors unvirtuous?
What particular virtue, agreeable to extropians, is violated by the trade
of sex for money, power, or other short- or long-term gain?  How does such
trade prevent one from "flourishing"?

