 nv91-asa@nada.kth.se (Anders Sandberg) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-463:
>Maybe this also would work in a post-Singularity world: lesser entities
>perform (comparatively) simple services for the greater entities which 
>provide them with resources they cannot produce themselves. In fact, they 
>might be parts of the exoselves of the greater entities or even internal 
>(like we use some of our memes). However, there is always the problem 
>that they might be regarded as interchangeable by the host: "Oh dear, I 
>have stepped on an ant again... but there are so many of them!".

 My attempt to translate this into a situation that would be more realistic in
the post-singularity world is "Oh dear, I overwrote another file belonging to
someone else ... but there are so many files!". Because the normal resource
allocation mechanisms on filesystems are much more sophisticated than that
available for the surface of a mudball, I think the ant analogy is poor.
