Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 14:10:26 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-474] The Genetic Code Is Idiotic

>From extropians-request@extropy.org Mon Dec 12 13:19 EST 1994
>To: Extropians@extropy.org
>X-Original-To: extropians@extropy.org
>Subject: The Genetic Code Is Idiotic 
>X-Extropian-Date: Remailed on December 12, 374 P.N.O. [13:08:40 UTC]
>X-Message-Number: #94-12-470
>
>John Clark tells the story of Francis Crick designing an efficient
>encoding for DNA, which turns out to not be the one that Ma Nature
>settled on.  I'm not as surprised as John was that the truth turns out
>to be more error-prone than Crick's design.  It makes sense for DNA to
>evolve for evolvability.

I agree with the sentiment, but not in this instance.  There are many
ways for DNA to change, including crossover, mutation, transposons,
and duplication or elimination of segments.  These are "gentle"
changes compared to getting out of sync with the codon frame.
Such a disastrous mistake might not be important for evolution.

(There is at least one gene which codes for one protein product with
one frame, and a different protein when transcription begins one
bp off, but we think this is rare.)

