From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 11:36:28 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-475] PHYS: New model for gravity

Vince Kerchner writes:
>The most recent (12/3/94) issue of "Science News" contains an article
>describing a new modification to General Relativity made by Huseyin Yilmaz
>of Tufts University and Carroll Alley of the University of Maryland. ...
>I wonder how this theory, if verified, will affect ideas like entropy,
>negative matter, and traversable wormholes?

As long as this theory remains deterministic, it can't do much to
change our concepts of entropy.  Entropy is remarkably resiliant to
changes in other theories - basic statistical mechanics is pretty
clearly the physics theory most likely to outlast all the others we
now have.  It is what we know best about the foundations of physics.

Robin Hanson

