Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 10:56:04 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-478] SING: Post-Singularity Morals

 70544.1227@compuserve.com (Reilly Jones) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-443:
>Peter McCluskey wrote 12/10/94:
><RJ: Never trust someone else's memories, you know how garbled your own are.
>
>PM: I routinely rely on my bank's recollection of how much money I have in my
>account, my internet access provider's recollection of my .newsrc, my compiler's
>recollection of the C++ grammar, etc. They are generally less garbled than the
>memories in my brain.>
>
>I don't think you mean your bank teller's memory, or your provider's
>administrator's memory, or your compiler's programmer's memory.  These are
>garbled.  You are referring to informational representations of memory, not an
>individual's unique memory itself.  I interpreted the point being made to be
>sharing actual subjective memories, not consensual representations of such
>memories.

 Digital memories are more reliable than neurons, but I generally don't care
what mechanism entities I rely on use for memory, I simply ask whether they
have a reputation for reliability. When I go to a restaurant, I am probably
relying on a chef's subjective memory of how to cook. My point is that I
expect the difference between that and my personal memory of how to cook
will lose most of its significance. It will be like asking who has read
and write access to a file.

>Peter again: <Those who worship a static form of identity to the extent that
>they fear significant self-improvement, or who don't trust anyone enough to rely
>on thoughts they didn't generate personally, will be limited to the kind of
>growth a hermit experiences.>
>
>Both likely true, but not how I interpreted the point being made.  I wasn't
>interpreting "memories" in the context I took Phil Goetz to be using them, as
>being merely informational representations of subjective memories, but actually
>sharing subjective memories themselves.  The sharing of subjective memories is
>self-immolation, since the material substrate of subjective memory is always
>unique.

 Imagine a computer program whose code is entirely self-contained. Imagine
that its square root function (its memory of how to calculate a square root)
is then replaced by a call to a routine in a shared library. Has this program
been immolated?
