From: CurtAdams@aol.com
Date: Mon, 12 Dec 1994 15:21:18 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-479] The Genetic Code Is Idiotic

John K Clark (johnkc@well.sf.ca.us) writes

>Crick devised a code that if read in the correct phase all the 
>triplets make sense, if read out of phase all the triplets would 
>be nonsense, that is, they wouldn't map to any amino acid. 
>Now the cell would know when it was reading RNA out of phase. 
>He thought he understood why life only used 20 amino acids, 
>his scheme would work with 20 amino acids in wouldn't work with 21. 
>Cricks code was simple, efficient, elegant, fault tolerant and 
>completely wrong. 

>The real genetic code was found by experiment a few years later.
>Incredibly the ribosome really does just start at the beginning
>and plod along 3 at a time, if it gets out of phase and makes
>the wrong protein, well, that's life in the big city. It's a
>pity Francis Crick wasn't around 4 billion years ago ; so much
>for "you can't improve on nature".
  
 The relative ease with which the code makes errors is not necessarily a
disadvantage.  There's nobody designing these proteins - they have to arise
by evolution, which in this case requires at least occaisional errors.

Some viruses (including our dear friend the HIV virus :-( ) take advantage of
the code by having some sections code simultaneously for two different
protein in different phases. 

Naturally now that WE can design proteins, "error-proofing" the code makes
eminent sense.

Curt Adams
